\description{
\code{simulate_joinme_mix()} is the generative counterpart of \code{\link[=joinme_mix]{joinme_mix()}}.
It follows the ordinary \code{\link[=simulate_joinme]{simulate_joinme()}} workflow, but replaces selected
standardised random-effect coordinates by a finite location--scale mixture.
The selected coordinates, class-regression formulae, ordering convention
and shared-allocation rules have the same meaning as in \code{\link[=joinme_mix]{joinme_mix()}}.

The function deliberately delegates observation-family sampling,
distributional regression, marker-weight construction, event-time
generation, censoring, delayed entry and association transformations to
\code{\link[=simulate_joinme]{simulate_joinme()}}. Only the distribution of the selected standardised
latent coordinates is changed. This provides a direct simulation-to-fit
workflow without maintaining a second longitudinal or survival simulator.
Population parameters follow the same rule as \code{\link[=simulate_joinme]{simulate_joinme()}}: numeric
declarations in \code{truth} are fixed exactly and \verb{prior_*()} declarations are
drawn once. The realised values are held fixed for the simulated data set
and stored in \code{truth}. This includes shared or association-term-specific
marker-weight locations. Class probabilities, regression coefficients,
locations and scales are recorded under \code{truth$mixture}.
}
\details{
Let \eqn{C_j\in\{1,\ldots,G\}} denote the latent class for allocation unit
\eqn{j}. Conditional on class \eqn{g}, every selected coordinate \eqn{r} is drawn
independently as

\deqn{
z_{jr}\mid C_j=g \sim
D\{\mu_{gr},\sigma_{gr}\},
}

where \eqn{D} is Student-\eqn{t_6}, Laplace or Normal according to \code{shrinkage}.
Unselected subject, marker and covariance-regression coordinates remain
standard Normal.

Marker weights are not latent-class coordinates. When they are estimated,
their common location is supplied by \code{truth$marker_weights$intercept},
and direct marker departures are drawn from the centred unit-scale
\code{truth$marker_weights$family}. No further scale multiplies those
departures because the association slope already scales the weighted
marker feature. The family name \code{"student_t"} draws one set-specific
value as \code{2 + Gamma(2, 0.1)}. The realised values are retained in \code{truth}. The departure
coordinate retains location zero and ordinary scale one. A constant family
bypasses both quantities, so the effective weights equal the offsets declared
in \code{truth$marker_weights} exactly.

Subject and covariance-regression coordinates share one subject-level
allocation. Combining compatible types consequently retains exactly \code{n_classes}
components rather than constructing a Cartesian product.

\code{formulaClass} is evaluated using the same allocation-unit design builder
as \code{\link[=joinme_mix]{joinme_mix()}}. A shared formula is applied to every active allocation
domain. A named list may supply separate \code{subject} and \code{marker}
formulae. A list of exactly \code{n_classes} formulae supplies class-specific
predictors. The formula intercept is omitted because \code{probability} in
\code{class_parameters} already specifies the zero-covariate probabilities.

\code{class_parameters} is a named list with the following entries:
\itemize{
\item \code{probability}: a positive vector of length \code{n_classes}, normalised to sum
to one. If omitted, positive values are drawn once and normalised; under
probability ordering the realised probabilities are sorted increasingly.
\item \code{location}: an \code{n_classes} by \(K\) matrix in the packed coordinate
order, or a named list containing matrices for selected class
types. Omitted locations are drawn once around separated class centres;
the ordered coordinate is sorted when intercept ordering is requested.
\item \code{scale}: a positive scalar, vector, matrix, or named level list matching
\code{location}. Omitted component scales are drawn once from \code{Uniform(0.45, 0.85)}.
}

The returned \code{truth$mixture} record contains the realised class
probabilities, allocations, component parameters, coordinate layout and
standardised latent draws. Its \code{stan_data_contract} entry records the exact
mixture dimensions, source indices, packed starts, ordering code and
class-design segments expected from a corresponding \code{joinme_mix(..., fit = FALSE)} call. Class labels are kept out of \code{dataLong} and \code{dataEvent}
so that an analysis cannot accidentally use the simulated truth as an
observed predictor.

A longitudinal-only simulation is requested with \code{formulaEvent = NULL},
matching \code{\link[=joinme_mix]{joinme_mix()}}. The common engine then uses an internal
zero-hazard scaffold solely to construct covariates and observation rows;
the returned \code{dataEvent} and survival formula are \code{NULL}. Explicit
association terms are not permitted in this mode.

The \code{truth} argument is created with \code{\link[=jm_truth]{jm_truth()}} and follows the scientific
component hierarchy used for fitting. It describes data-generating
quantities rather than fitting distributions. In particular,
class truths are declared together as
\code{class = list(baseline_prob = ..., slope = ...)}: \code{baseline_prob} governs
the Dirichlet prior and \code{slope} governs coefficients introduced by
\code{formulaClass}. A numeric \code{class$slope} fixes the generating coefficient
vector; a \verb{prior_*()} declaration draws it once at the beginning of the
simulation. Class probabilities, locations and scales are supplied through
\code{class_parameters} when fixed generating values are required.
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
simulated <- simulate_joinme_mix(
  formulaLong = y ~ 1 + time + x1 +
    (1 + time | id) +
    (0 + x1 + (1 + time | id) | marker),
  formulaEvent = survival::Surv(time, event) ~ x1 + x2,
  n_id = 80,
  families = c("gaussian", "student_t", "gaussian"),
  n_classes = 3,
  formulaClass = ~ x1,
  class_type = c("subject", "vcov"),
  truth = jm_truth(class = list(slope = c(
    "class_1:x1" = -0.6,
    "class_2:x1" = 0.4
  ))),
  class_parameters = list(
    probability = c(0.25, 0.45, 0.30),
    location = matrix(
      c(-1.5, -0.8, 0, 0, 1.5, 0.8),
      nrow = 3,
      byrow = TRUE
    ),
    scale = 0.7
  ),
  seed = 701
)

fitted <- joinme_mix(
  formulaLong = simulated$truth$formulaLong,
  dataLong = simulated$dataLong,
  formulaEvent = simulated$truth$formulaEvent,
  dataEvent = simulated$dataEvent,
  formulaVCov = simulated$truth$formulaVCov,
  families = simulated$marker_info$families,
  n_classes = 3,
  formulaClass = ~ x1,
  class_type = c("subject", "vcov")
)
}
}
